{"black_box_warning":{"current_value":"No current FDA/DailyMed label identified.","fda_source_urls":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2016/022345s011lbl.pdf","https://www.fda.gov/drugs/drug-safety-and-availability/fda-drug-safety-communication-fda-determines-2013-labeling-adequate-manage-risk-retinal"],"proposed_value":"Boxed warning in last FDA-approved Potiga label: retinal abnormalities and potential vision loss.","rationale":"The 2016 FDA Potiga label contains a boxed warning for retinal abnormalities and potential vision loss, and FDA's 2015 Drug Safety Communication explicitly refers to recommendations in the Boxed Warning. DailyMed should not be cited for this field.","requires_user_approval":true,"status":"incorrect"},"blocking_questions":[],"fact_checks":[{"current_value":"ezogabine","field":"generic_name","proposed_value":"ezogabine","rationale":"FDA labeling, FDA safety communication, and NCBI LiverTox identify Potiga as ezogabine.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2016/022345s011lbl.pdf","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548304/"]},{"current_value":"retigabine","field":"alternate_generic_names","proposed_value":"retigabine","rationale":"NCBI LiverTox states ezogabine is known as retigabine in Europe, and ILAE/GSK notes the same medicine is called Potiga/ezogabine in the United States and Trobalt/retigabine elsewhere. This supports treating retigabine as an alias, not a duplicate row.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548304/","https://www.ilae.org/files/dmfile/GSK_RetigabineTrobalt-Reminder.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Potiga; Trobalt","field":"trade_names","proposed_value":"Potiga; Trobalt","rationale":"NCBI LiverTox identifies Potiga in the United States and Trobalt in Europe/elsewhere; the ILAE/GSK discontinuation notice uses Trobalt/Potiga for the same medicine.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548304/","https://www.ilae.org/files/dmfile/GSK_RetigabineTrobalt-Reminder.pdf"]},{"current_value":"retigabine","field":"pubmed_search_aliases","proposed_value":"retigabine","rationale":"The RCT literature uses retigabine and ezogabine/retigabine naming; retigabine should remain a PubMed search alias.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27376872/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21451152/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20944074/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17420403/"]},{"current_value":"No - formerly FDA-approved/marketed; withdrawn/discontinued","field":"available_in_us","proposed_value":"No - formerly FDA-approved/marketed; withdrawn/discontinued","rationale":"ILAE/GSK states Potiga/ezogabine in the United States would be discontinued on the same June 2017 timeline as Trobalt; FDA labeling confirms historical approval.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.ilae.org/files/dmfile/GSK_RetigabineTrobalt-Reminder.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2016/022345s011lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Focal","field":"epilepsy_type","proposed_value":"Focal","rationale":"The FDA label indication is partial-onset seizures, the older term corresponding to focal seizures.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2016/022345s011lbl.pdf","https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/overview/trobalt-epar-summary-public_en.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Tablet","field":"formulations_available","proposed_value":"Tablet","rationale":"FDA labeling lists Potiga tablets in 50, 200, 300, and 400 mg strengths; EMA lists Trobalt tablets.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2016/022345s011lbl.pdf","https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/overview/trobalt-epar-summary-public_en.pdf"]},{"current_value":"7-11 h","field":"half_life_range","proposed_value":"7-11 h","rationale":"FDA labeling states ezogabine and its N-acetyl metabolite have elimination half-lives of 7 to 11 hours.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2016/022345s011lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Liver","field":"major_organ_for_metabolism","proposed_value":"Liver","rationale":"FDA labeling and NCBI LiverTox describe extensive metabolism by glucuronidation and acetylation; renal excretion is the major elimination route, but the metabolism field is supported as hepatic/liver.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2016/022345s011lbl.pdf","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548304/"]},{"current_value":"Not a major CYP inducer/inhibitor","field":"enzyme_inducing_or_inhibiting","proposed_value":"Not a major CYP inducer/inhibitor","rationale":"FDA labeling states in vitro studies showed no oxidative metabolism by CYP enzymes and CYP inhibitors/inducers are unlikely to affect ezogabine/NAMR pharmacokinetics; LiverTox states metabolism is independent of microsomal P450 and lacks significant drug interactions.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2016/022345s011lbl.pdf","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548304/"]},{"current_value":"Inhibitor","field":"filter_enzyme_effect","proposed_value":"None/Not a major inducer or inhibitor","rationale":"The row's filter conflicts with the populated enzyme fact and FDA/NCBI sources; ezogabine is not a major CYP inhibitor.","requires_user_approval":true,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2016/022345s011lbl.pdf","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548304/"]},{"current_value":"1200 mg/day","field":"maximum_approved_daily_dose","proposed_value":"1200 mg/day","rationale":"FDA label maximum general adult dosage is 400 mg three times daily, 1,200 mg/day.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2016/022345s011lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"600 mg/day","field":"minimum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"600 mg/day","rationale":"FDA label maintenance dosage begins at 600 mg/day; FDA statistical review notes 600 mg/day was statistically superior to placebo in Study 302.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2016/022345s011lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/nda/2011/022345Orig1s000StatR.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Withdrawn; historical adult dose 600-1200 mg/day divided TID","field":"typical_doses_per_day","proposed_value":"Withdrawn; historical adult dose 600-1200 mg/day divided TID","rationale":"FDA labeling supports 600-1,200 mg/day in three divided doses; ILAE/GSK supports withdrawal/discontinuation.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2016/022345s011lbl.pdf","https://www.ilae.org/files/dmfile/GSK_RetigabineTrobalt-Reminder.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Neuronal potassium-channel opener; primarily activates Kv7/KCNQ channels, stabilizing resting membrane potential and reducing neuronal excitability.","field":"mechanism_of_action","proposed_value":"Neuronal potassium-channel opener; primarily activates Kv7/KCNQ channels, stabilizing resting membrane potential and reducing neuronal excitability.","rationale":"EMA explains retigabine keeps neuronal potassium channels open to prevent transmission of electrical impulses; Sills/Rogawski classify retigabine/ezogabine as a voltage-gated potassium-channel modulator.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/overview/trobalt-epar-summary-public_en.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32120063/","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548304/"]},{"current_value":"EMA Trobalt withdrawal page; Sills and Rogawski 2020 ASM mechanism review","field":"mechanism_source","proposed_value":"EMA Trobalt EPAR summary; Sills and Rogawski 2020 ASM mechanism review; NCBI LiverTox Ezogabine","rationale":"The EMA withdrawal page supports authorization withdrawal but not mechanism. EMA's EPAR summary, Sills/Rogawski, and LiverTox support the potassium-channel mechanism.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/overview/trobalt-epar-summary-public_en.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32120063/","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548304/"]},{"current_value":"High","field":"mechanism_confidence","proposed_value":"High","rationale":"Mechanism is consistently supported by EMA, NCBI LiverTox, and peer-reviewed review literature.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/overview/trobalt-epar-summary-public_en.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32120063/","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548304/"]},{"current_value":"EMA/UK SmPC","field":"mechanism_source_tier","proposed_value":"EMA EPAR/peer-reviewed review","rationale":"The row cites an EMA EPAR-type source and a peer-reviewed mechanism review, not a UK SmPC located during audit.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/overview/trobalt-epar-summary-public_en.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32120063/"]},{"current_value":"CNS: dizziness 23%, CNS: somnolence 22%, constitutional: fatigue 15%, confusional state 9%, urologic: urinary retention 2%","field":"adverse_symptoms_percentages","proposed_value":"CNS: dizziness 23%, CNS: somnolence 22%, constitutional: fatigue 15%, confusional state 9%, urologic: urinary retention approximately 2%","rationale":"FDA label supports dizziness 23%, somnolence 22%, fatigue 15%, confusional state 9%, and urinary retention in approximately 2% of the epilepsy safety database. Minor wording change clarifies that urinary retention is approximate and from a broader database, not the Table 4 all-dose column.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2016/022345s011lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"QT prolongation not typical; urinary/retinal toxicity drove withdrawal","field":"qt_interval_effect","proposed_value":"QT prolongation possible; FDA label reports mean 7.7-msec QT prolongation at 400 mg TID and recommends monitoring in at-risk patients. Global discontinuation was attributed by GSK/EMA to limited use/commercial reasons, not efficacy or safety.","rationale":"FDA labeling documents a QT interval effect. The withdrawal-causation statement is contradicted by ILAE/GSK and EMA withdrawal notices, which state permanent discontinuation/withdrawal was for limited usage/commercial reasons.","requires_user_approval":true,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2016/022345s011lbl.pdf","https://www.ilae.org/files/dmfile/GSK_RetigabineTrobalt-Reminder.pdf","https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/public-statement/public-statement-trobalt-withdrawal-marketing-authorisation-european-union_en.pdf"]},{"current_value":"QT prolongation","field":"filter_qt_effect","proposed_value":"QT prolongation","rationale":"FDA labeling supports a QT interval effect, including a mean 7.7-msec QT prolongation at 400 mg TID.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2016/022345s011lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"2011 FDA approval; EMA Trobalt/retigabine marketing authorisation granted 2011 and withdrawn 2018","field":"year_fda_cleared","proposed_value":"2011 FDA approval; EMA Trobalt/retigabine marketing authorisation granted 28 Mar 2011 and withdrawn 19 Jul 2018","rationale":"The current value is directionally correct; exact EMA dates improve precision.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2016/022345s011lbl.pdf","https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/public-statement/public-statement-trobalt-withdrawal-marketing-authorisation-european-union_en.pdf"]},{"current_value":"NCBI LiverTox anticonvulsants table; EMA Trobalt withdrawal page; ILAE/GSK discontinuation notice; Sills and Rogawski 2020 ASM mechanism review; FDA/DailyMed labeling","field":"evidence_sources","proposed_value":"FDA Potiga prescribing information; FDA Potiga Drug Safety Communication; NCBI LiverTox Ezogabine; EMA Trobalt EPAR summary and withdrawal statement; ILAE/GSK Trobalt/Potiga discontinuation notice; Sills and Rogawski 2020 ASM mechanism review; cited PubMed RCTs","rationale":"DailyMed should not be used for black-box verification, and the mechanism is supported by EMA EPAR rather than the EMA withdrawal page. FDA labeling and FDA safety communication should be named explicitly.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2016/022345s011lbl.pdf","https://www.fda.gov/drugs/drug-safety-and-availability/fda-drug-safety-communication-fda-determines-2013-labeling-adequate-manage-risk-retinal","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548304/","https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/overview/trobalt-epar-summary-public_en.pdf","https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/public-statement/public-statement-trobalt-withdrawal-marketing-authorisation-european-union_en.pdf","https://www.ilae.org/files/dmfile/GSK_RetigabineTrobalt-Reminder.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.n